answer.answer:
PSP had serious usability problems that couldn't be fixed without breaking changes, so it was deprecated (in v1.21) instead of being promoted to GA. The problems were:

- **Confusing authorization model.** Policies were bound to either the requesting user or the pod's service account. Granting permission to the user is intuitive but breaks controllers, and the dual model weakens security. It was easy to grant broader permissions than intended, and hard to see which PSP applied in a given situation.
- **Risky rollout.** PSP fails closed when no policy exists, so it needed 100% coverage before being turned on. With no dry-run or audit mode, retrofitting it onto existing clusters was impractical, and it could never be enabled by default.
- **Inconsistent, unbounded API.** The API grew organically and has many internal inconsistencies. It was unclear what belonged in PSP, it didn't compose well, and mutation priority could be unexpected. The "changing Pod defaults" feature only worked for some Pod settings, and it wasn't obvious when it would apply.

The community still wanted a built-in way to prevent privilege escalation through the create-pod permission. External admission controllers such as K-Rail, Kyverno and OPA/Gatekeeper already existed, so the replacement (KEP-2579, Pod Security Admission) didn't need to be all things to all people. It was designed instead to:

- be simple;
- have soft rollout features so it can be added to existing clusters;
- be configurable enough to eventually be active by default;
- be partly or fully deactivatable so it can coexist with external controllers.

PSP was removed in v1.25.
